what = IntroWhat(
  "Hexagonal Architecture (also known as Ports & Adapters) decouples core business domain logic from external dependencies like HTTP controllers, databases, message brokers, and third-party APIs through explicit contracts called Ports, implemented by Adapters.",
  "An architectural pattern originated by Alistair Cockburn that isolates application core logic using inbound and outbound Ports and Adapters.",
  [
    "Driving (Primary) Ports & Adapters handle incoming user requests.",
    "Driven (Secondary) Ports & Adapters handle outgoing persistence and external service calls.",
    "Domain logic stays pure with zero framework dependencies.",
  ],
)

why = IntroWhy(
  "Traditional layered architectures tend to leak database entities and framework concerns into application code, making software hard to test, refactor, or migrate. Ports & Adapters allow developers to test business logic completely in memory without databases or HTTP servers.",
  "Enables fast in-memory unit testing and painless framework migrations.",
)
